KEYWORDS: metal−organic frameworks, pore partition agent, nanozymes, peroxidase-like activity, enzymatic cascade reaction, chemiluminescence, extracellular vesicles
E nzymatic cascade reactions involve two or more enzymes successively in one reactor. They offer high reaction efficiency, because of the enrichment of reactants localizing nearby the enzymes, complete mass transfer between different enzymatic reactions, and reduce intermediate decomposition.1,2 Recently, extensive works have been focused on substituting the natural enzymes with the enzyme-mimicking nanomaterials, that is, nanozymes,3 in multienzyme reactions for their applications in detection of diverse biomarkers,4 including glucose,5−7 acetylcholine,8 viral antigens,9 and tumor proteins,10 because of their high activity, superior tunability, and low cost.3,4 Among different designs of nanozymes, Metal−Organic Frameworks (MOFs) are one unique group of materials that deliver many advantages: they have abundant active catalytic sites per unit volume of the material, are capable of generating high signals through enzymatic reactions; and they can be formed by a variety of chemicals, including those with good biocompatibility as well as rich functional groups for biomolecule conjugation.11−13 Furthermore, the high specific surface area, tunable pore size, and adjustable surface property of MOFs can assist with target separation and enrichment, as demonstrated in separation of biomolecules like DNA,14 peptide,15 and exosome,16 from the complex sample matrix by MOFs with high stability in aqueous environments. With the dual-functionality of target separation and signal production, MOFs are ideal choices to construct sensing platforms for diverse analytes.
In our previous work,17 we developed the bimetallic MOF of $\mathrm{Fe/Co-MIL}{\cdot}88\mathrm{(NH_{2})}$ and demonstrated that the addition of ${\bf C o}(\mathrm{II})$ can greatly enhance the peroxidase-like activity of the MOF compared to the monometallic Fe-MIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ , owing to the synergistic action of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})$ and ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{III})$ in the Fentonlike reactions. The peroxidase-mimicking MOF can be coupled with glucose oxidase $\left(\operatorname{GOx}\right)$ to form the cascade reaction system, and be applied for colorimetric detection of a promising biomarker family, extracellular vesicles (EVs)18 with a limit of detection (LOD) $\sim1\times10^{5}$ EV particles/mL. However, the dynamic range of detection using Fe/Co-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ is very narrow, spanning over only 1 order of magnitude. We suspect that, since the abundant unsaturated metal sites in the structure of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ MOF are susceptible to the attack from Lewis bases, this material may be unstable in aqueous solutions and complex matrices that contain rich chelators, and may not stand the burst production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in proximity when the analyte concentrations are high.
A pore space partition strategy that entails the insertion of a pore-partition agent in the framework has been applied to the MIL-88 (acs topology net) series MOF to produce the pacs (partitioned acs) MOFs with much improved stability and performance for gas separation.19−22 Herein, we introduce the pore partition agent, 2,4,6-tris(4-pyridyl)pyridine (TPY), to the bimetallic Fe/Co-MIL-88 $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ) MOF to form the $\mathrm{Fe/Co^{-}}$ TPY-MIL-88( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) MOF. We prove that this material possesses several advantages compared to the no-TPY counterpart: (1) it is much more porous and shows ${>}16$ -fold higher surface area per unit mass; (2) it exhibits much higher stability in basic solutions and in protein-rich matrices, like serum, while maintaining high peroxidase-like activity; (3) it can catalyze chemiluminescence (CL) production from luminol at $\mathtt{p H\ 10}$ as a peroxidase-mimic, which cannot be done with the no-TPY MOF, and the resultant CL can last for tens of minutes; and (4) when applied to detect EVs, it increases the dynamic range by 1 order of magnitude.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials. Ferric(III) chloride, 6-hydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $98.1\%$ ) was acquired from J. T. Baker (Phillipsburg, NJ). Cobalt(II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $99\%$ ) was purchased from Acros. Sodium chloride, potassium phosphate dibasic salt (anhydrous), acetic acid, $N,N.$ -dimethylacetamide (DMA), and $N,N_{\astrosun}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) were purchased from Fisher Scientific (Waltham, MA). The following chemicals, 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})\right)$ - BDC), $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), potassium chloride, glycine (for electrophoresis, $\geq99\%$ ), and glutaraldehyde, were from Sigma-Aldrich (Saint Louis, MI). Chemicals 2,4,6-tris(4-pyridyl)- pyridine (TPY) and 1,3-dimethyl-3,4,5,6-tetrahydro-2(1H)-pyrimidinone $\mathbf{\widetilde{\mathbf{\xi}}}N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylpropyleneurea, DMPU) were purchased from TCI; and $^{1,1,1,5,5,5}$ -hexafluoro-2,4-pentanedione (HFP) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. Ethanol (EtOH, Anhydrous) was from KOPTEC. mPEG- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ was purchased from Abbexa Ltd (Houston, Texas, USA).The amino group modified CD63 aptamer was produced by IDT, Inc. (Coralville, IA). The mouse antihuman CD9 (Clone MM2/57) and HRP were obtained from Sino Biological (Beijing, China) and Cell Signaling Technology (Danvers, MA), respectively. Streptavidin was purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific (Waltham, MA). All chemicals were at the analytical reagent grade and used without further purification. Ultrapure water with electric resistance $>18.2\ \mathrm{M}\Omega$ was produced by the Millipore Milli-Q water purification system (Billerica, MA).
Synthesis and Activation of $\mathsf{F e/C o-T P Y-M l L-88(N H_{2})}$ . The MOF materials were prepared via a solvothermal method. In a typical approach for the production of the bimetallic Fe/Co-TPY- MIL${88}\mathrm{(NH_{2})}$ , $0.041\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{FeCl_{3}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O},$ $0.042\textrm{g C o}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $0.030\mathrm{~g~}$ TPY, and $0.056\mathrm{~g~H_{2}(N H_{2})B D C}$ were dissolved in $4.007\ \mathrm{g}$ DMA and $2.001\mathrm{~g~}$ DMPU in a $23~\mathrm{mL}$ glass bottle, then added 6 small drops HFP, stirred for $\sim40\ \mathrm{min,}$ and then heated in $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After being cooled to room temperature, the brown-color crystals $\mathrm{Fe/Co}.$ -
TPY-MIL- $\cdot88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ were washed with DMF or DMA, collected by centrifugation, and dried at room temperature. MOF crystals were washed twice with ethanol at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or room temperature and then dried by vacuum to remove the residual solvents that may impede the subsequent enzyme activity measurement and sensing performance. Fe/Co-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ was prepared as literature described (condition $1)^{17}$ to be compared with the newly designed Fe/Co-TPY-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ for stability, activity, and sensing performance. Fe/Co-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ used for $\mathrm{N}_{2}>77\mathrm{K}$ adsorption test was prepared by condition 2, which was slightly different from condition 1 (see Figure S4 caption text). Both samples synthesized by condition 1 and condition 2 have $\mathrm{Fe/Co-MIL{-}}88\mathrm{(NH_{2})}$ structural topology as verified by Powder X-ray Diffraction.
Characterization of Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ . The phase purity of Fe/Co-TPY-MIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ was examined by Powder X-ray Diffraction (PXRD). Most of the Powder X-ray diffraction experiments were performed on a Bruker D2 Phaser diffractometer, which was operating at $30\ \mathrm{kV}$ and $10~\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\alpha$ radiation, $\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). The data collection was performed at room temperature in the range from $5^{\circ}$ to $40^{\circ}$ with a step size of ${\sim}0.020^{\circ}$ . The PXRD experiments in Figure S3b were performed on a different instrument (see Figure S3 caption text). The simulated PXRD of Fe/Co-TPY-MIL- $8\bar{8}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ was obtained from the Fe-TPY-MIL-88(NH2) single crystal data.23 Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM) was done by the Thermo Scientific Talos L120C TEM equipment with Thermo Scientific CETA $16~4\mathrm{K}\times4\mathrm{K}$ CMOS camera. Zeta-potential measurement and dynamic light scattering (DLS) were performed on a Zetasizer nano ZS90 (Malvern Instruments).
Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area was measured by the $\Nu_{2}77\mathrm{~K~}$ adsorption isotherm carried out on Micromeritics ASAP 2020 PLUS Physisorption Analyzer, and BET surface area was calculated from the adsorption isotherm by the BET equation (The points chosen to ensure that C always ${>}0$ , and correlation coefficient always ${\tt>}0.999{\tt\dot{\mathrm{\Omega}}}$ ). For the Fe/Co-TPY-MIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ MOF, the material was immersed in ethanol for ${\sim}3$ days, with the solution exchanged to a fresh preparation every day, and then degassed under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for ${\sim}12.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . For the Fe/Co-MIL-88 $\mathrm{\tilde{NH}}_{2})$ MOF, after the same ethanol treatment, it was dried by vacuum and then degassed under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for ${\sim}14\mathrm{~h~}$ .
MOF stability in different $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ was evaluated by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES). The MOF of 2.5 mg was suspended in $12.5~\mathrm{mL}$ buffer for $15~\mathrm{min}$ , and the supernatant was collected and mixed with $0.269\mathrm{\mL}$ $70\%$ nitric acid, $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of which was used for ICP-OES analysis.
Peroxidase-Like Activity Evaluation and Enzyme Kinetics Analysis of $F e/C o-T P Y-M l L-88(N H_{2})$ . The peroxidase-like activity of the MOF materials was evaluated using the reaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB). In brief, $50~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ MOF, $1~\mathrm{mM}$ TMB, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at $\mathbf{1}\mathrm{mM}$ were added to $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of the NaAcHAc buffer ( $200~\mathrm{mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}4.1$ ). The mixture was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\ \mathrm{min}.$ , and the visible light absorbance at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ of the TMB oxidation product, that is, oxTMB, was read in the BioTek Synergy HT microplate reader (Agilent).
The steady-state kinetics assays were performed as follows. The Fe/ Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left\langle\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right\rangle$ ) going through the activation process was prepared at the concentration of $50~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ in $100~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $200\ \mathrm{mM}$ NaAc-HAc buffer $\left(\mathsf{p H4.1}\right)$ . Then $1.0~\mathrm{mM}$ TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at various concentrations (0.005, 0.05, 0.1, 0.5, 1.0, and $10\ \mathrm{mM}$ ) were added and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . A time-course mode was used to monitor the absorbance change at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with a $10\ s$ interval for $15~\mathrm{min}$ in the BioTek Synergy HT microplate reader. The Beer−Lambert law was used to convert the absorbance to oxTMB concentration using the molar absorptivity at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ of oxTMB (39, $000~\mathrm{M}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1})^{2\mp}$ and the optical length of $0.233~\mathrm{cm}$ of the 96 well plates. The concentration change at unit time within the first $15\mathrm{min}$ period was then calculated to represent the initial velocity (V).
Preparation of Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ /Aptamer Bioconjugate. Aptamer conjugation started by dispersing 35 mg Fe/Co-TPYMIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ in $20~\mathrm{mL}~1\times\$ PBS and adding $100~\mu\mathrm{L}$ glutaraldehyde. After stirring the solution for $2\textrm{h}$ , the solid was centrifuged, washed with $1\times$ PBS 2 times, and dispersed in $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}1\times$ PBS. Next, $10\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.1~\mathrm{mM}$ aptamer was added to the mixture, and the solution was put on a rotating plate and shaken overnight. The Fe/Co-TPY-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big),$ /aptamer conjugate was then collected by centrifugation $(20,000g,\ 3\ \mathrm{\min})$ ) and redispersed in $2\ \mathrm{~mL~}0.1\ \mathrm{~M~}$ glycine buffer containing $32~\mathrm{mM}$ PEG $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ to passivate the MOF surface.
EV Detection Using Fe/Co-TPY-MIL- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}88(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ in Enzyme Cascades. Twenty $\mu\mathrm{L}$ of the EV sample was mixed with $170~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of the Fe/Co-TPY- MIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ /aptamer bioconjugate at $17.5\mathrm{\mg/}$ $\mathrm{\mL}$ and $10\mu\mathrm{L}$ of the biotin-anti-CD9 antibody (or biotin-anti-HER2 antibody) solution at $0.10~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ . The mixture was shaken at room temperature for $3\mathrm{h}$ . The MOFs with the bound EVs and proteins was centrifuged down $\left(20000g,3\mathrm{\min}\right)$ ) and resuspended in $100\mu\mathrm{L}$ of the streptavidin solution at $0.017~\mathrm{\mg/mL}$ . After 1-h incubation, the product was centrifuged down $(20000g,3\mathrm{min})$ , washed with $1\times{\mathrm{PBS}}$ once, and mixed with $100~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of the biotinylated $\mathbf{GOx}$ at $0.013~\mathrm{mg/}$ mL. After two washes with $\mathbf{\mathrm{1\timesPBS}}$ , the final product was resuspended in $1\times$ PBS, $25~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of which was mixed with $25~\mu\mathrm{L}$ luminol (0.128 mM). Fifty $\mu\mathrm{L}$ glucose $(2\mathrm{mM})$ was injected into mixture before being tested in the Promega GloMax Multiplus Plate Reader. Luminescence was collected within the wavelength range of $350{-}650~\mathrm{nm}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSIONS
Design and Characterization of the Bimetallic Fe/CoTPY-MIL-88 $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ . Many MOFs have been found to possess high peroxidase-mimicking activity and are capable of generating high signals to enable sensing of low-abundant biomarkers, as what we reported previously for the Fe/Co$\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ MOF.17 However, one big hurdle in applying MOFs in biomedical fields, including in biosensing, is that they contain unsaturated, open metal sites, which could be easily attacked by electron-rich ligands such as water molecule, hydroxide anion and biomolecules in clinical samples, reducing the MOF stability in such environments. It has been reported that, with the insertion of a second ligand, more metal−ligand bonds could be formed to block the open metal sites and improve the stability of MOF.25−27 The second ligand should match the size and symmetry of the geometrical pattern of the framework coordination sites and be compatible with the dimension of the channels formed by the first ligand to realize pore space partition. In $\mathrm{Fe/Co-MIL{-}}88\mathrm{(NH_{2})}$ , the unsaturated, open metal sites could form three metal−ligand bonds with three different ligand atoms on each layer along the ab plane. These metal−ligand bonds are in the manner of $C_{3}$ symmetry and point to the center of the hexagonal channel. A planar tritopic ligand with $C_{3}$ symmetry like TPY is ideal to fit such geometric features that to saturate the open metal sites,19 and thus was chosen in the present work to enhance the stability of the bimetallic MOF (Figure S1) and improve its performance in biosensing.
The bimetallic Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left\langle\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right\rangle$ was then synthesized by the solvothermal method, as described in the Experimental Section. Fe(III) and ${\bf C o}(\mathrm{II})$ can coordinate with the carboxyl groups on the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}$ ligands, forming the trinuclear transition metal (TM) clusters $\mathrm{'TM}=\mathrm{Fe}$ and Co). In addition, each TPY ligand coordinates with the three metal ions by its three pyridyl ends, giving a rigid framework (Figure 1a). The as-synthesized Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ MOF was initially distributed in the organic solvents used for synthesis. The stagnant organic solvent that stayed in the pore space of MOF was removed by soaking the MOF in ethanol and then drying it in vacuum. This process is termed activation, which did not alter the MOF size and structure (Figure S3), and the “activated” materials were characterized and used throughout the present work.
Examination by TEM reveals that the Fe/Co-TPY-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ MOF exhibits a uniform hexagon shape with the edge length about $500\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1b); and DLS measurement in distilled water gives out an average hydrodynamic diameter of $1.8\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S3a and Table S1). It is not expected that the hydration size measured by DLS for nonspherical particles would match exactly with that from TEM which has no solvent present.28 Still, the much larger hydration size observed in DLS likely reflects some degrees of aggregation of the $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ -TPY$\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ MOF in the aqueous environment: the zetapotential of the TPY MOF was lower than that of the no-TPY counterpart (Table S1).
PXRD was performed to characterize the crystal structure. The peaks of the $\mathrm{Fe/Co\mathrm{-}T P Y\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}88(N H_{2})}$ match well with those of the simulated Fe-TPY-MIL-88 $\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) spectrum, confirming that the synthesized Fe/Co-TPY-MIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ shares the same topology with Fe-TPY-MIL-88( $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) (Figure 1c). In addition, the PXRD patterns of the MIL-88 MOF with or without TPY (Figure 1c and Figure S4a) show distinct differences. For Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , four characteristic peaks at the low angles, (010), (011), $(-120)$ , and (002), are present, indicating the MOF belongs to the pacs class.19−22 These peaks are different from the PXRD of the no-TPY MOF. Moreover, the BET surface area of Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ was found to be $1300~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},\sim16$ times of that of the no-TPY MOF (Figure 1d). The high porosity of $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ -TPY-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ agrees well with the results reported in literature for the pacs MOF materials.29 Both the PXRD and BET data support that, TPY should have successfully participated in constructing the framework of Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left\langle{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}\right\rangle$ ).
Furthermore, we carried out the EDS line scan analysis (Figure S2) and confirmed the coexistence of Fe $(0.70\mathrm{~keV})$ and Co $(0.77\mathrm{keV})$ in the Fe/Co-TPY- MIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ framework. The atomic $\%$ of Fe and Co was found to be $1.65\%$ and $1.36\%$ , respectively, while the mole ratio of the two metals added to the synthesis solution was 1:1. The weight $\%$ of Fe was around $6.55\%$ , with that of Co being about $5.69\%$ .
Evaluation of the Peroxidase-Like Activity of Fe/CoTPY-MIL-88 $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ . To assess the impact on the peroxidaselike activity imposed by the addition of the second ligand TPY, we examined the model reaction of TMB oxidation into the blue oxTMB by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ catalyzed by Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ (Figure 2a). As seen from Figure S5, the visible light absorption at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for oxTMB changed from 0 to 0.075 with the addition of $50~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Fe/Co-TPY-MIL- $\cdot88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ to $1.0\ \mathrm{mM}$ TMB in $200\ \mathrm{mM}$ NaAc-HAc buffer at $\mathrm{pH4.l}$ ; and then to 0.41 with $1.0\mathrm{\mM}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ added. These results well prove that the $\mathrm{Fe/Co\mathrm{-}T P Y\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}88(N H_{2})}$ could act like a peroxidase to catalyze TMB oxidation by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The low signal produced without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is the result of oxidase-mimicking activity.30 Steady-state kinetic analysis was also carried out. The absorbance change at $652\ \mathrm{nm}$ was monitored with the MOF and one of the reactants (TMB or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ) added at fixed concentrations to react with varying concentrations of the other reactant. The initial production rate (V $\mathrm{in}\mu\mathrm{M.min}^{-1}$ ) of oxTMB within the first $15\ \mathrm{min}$ reaction window was plotted against the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations ([S]) to yield the Michaelis− Menten curve (Figure 2b). Rearranging Figure 2b to the linear Lineweaver−Burk plot (Figure S6) can attain the Michaelis constant $\left(K_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ and maximum reaction velocity $\left(V_{\mathrm{max}}\right)$ from its slope and Y-intercept: $\left[1/V=\left(K_{\mathrm{m}}/V_{\mathrm{max}}\right)\times\left(\mathrm{i}/\lbrack S\rbrack\right)+1/V_{\mathrm{max}}\right]$ . The molarity of the MOF was estimated by counting the number of MOF particles under an optical microscope, which was then divided by $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ (Figure 2c) to calculate the catalytic constant $(k_{\mathrm{cat}})$ of the bimetallic Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ . The $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $k_{\mathrm{{cat}}}$ against TMB, was also measured (Figure S7). We compared the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $k_{\mathrm{{cat}}}$ values with those of Fe/Co-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ acquired in our previous work.17 As shown in Figure 2c, a $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ of $0.697~\mathrm{mM}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was found for $\mathrm{Fe/Co}$ -TPYMIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ , close to that of $\mathrm{Fe/Co-MIL{-}}88\mathrm{(NH_{2})}$ ; but the $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ of Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ to TMB was about 4 times larger. This comparison indicates that blocking the open metal sites by ligand did not affect the affinity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to MOF, but reduced the affinity of TMB. This is probably due to the difference in TMB interaction with TPY and the original ligand of 2-aminoterephthalic acid, which has two carboxyl groups to interact with the primary amines on TMB.
Surprisingly, the $k_{\mathrm{{cat}}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe/Co\mathrm{-}T P Y\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}88(N H_{2})}$ measured with varying $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or TMB concentrations were $150-200$ times larger than those of Fe/Co-MIL-8 ${\mathsf{S}}({\mathrm{NH}}_{2})$ . One cause for the higher enzymatic activity could be its relatively higher amount of the more active metal of Co compared to $\mathrm{Fe/Co^{-}}$ $\mathrm{{MIL}}{\cdot}88\big(\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}\big)$ : the EDS results illustrate that the Fe: Co atom ratio in Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{2})$ was close to 1:1, while it was 3:1 in $\mathrm{Fe/Co-MIL{-}}88\mathrm{(NH_{2})}.$ . Additionally, by introducing TPY in Fe/Co-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , TPY occupies the open metal site on metal trimers and the Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88( $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ) MOF shows much enhanced stability, rigidity and porosity compared to Fe/Co-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , which is quite flexible with limited pore access without TPY.31−33 As a result, the BET surface area of $\mathrm{Fe/Co\mathrm{-}T P Y\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}88(N H_{2})}$ is $1300~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ much larger than that of $\mathrm{Fe/Co-MIL{-}}88\mathrm{(NH_{2})}$ MOF. The pore size distribution profile of the $\mathrm{Fe/Co\mathrm{-}T P Y\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}88(N H_{2})}$ MOF calculated with the Density Functional Theory (DFT) using the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption data is shown in Figure S4b. The Fe/CoTPY-MIL- $88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ MOF has a pore width distribution at around 5.9 Å (Figure S4b). With the more porous structure, the active metal site in the TPY MOF should be more accessible to the substrates (the topological polar surface area of TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is 52 and $40.{\bar{5}}{\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}}^{2}$ , respectively (https:// pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov)), enhancing the peroxidase-mimicking activity. On the other hand, $k_{c a t}$ indicates the product turnover rate generated per mole of the “enzyme” per second. Since the hydration size of the TPY MOF enlarged by ${\sim}2.5\cdot$ fold $(1.8\pm0.05\mu\mathrm{m}$ of the TPY MOF vs $0.70\pm0.1\mu\mathrm{m}$ of the no-TPY MOF, Table S1) compared to the no-TPY MOF, each mole of the TPY MOF would have more metals to result in the much larger $k_{\mathrm{{cat}}}$ value.
We then assessed the capability of the Fe/Co-TPY-MIL${88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)}$ to catalyze CL generation from luminol in $\mathsf{p H}$ ranging from 5 to 12. We mixed $50~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Fe/Co-TPY-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ with $1.0\mathrm{\mM\H_{2}O_{2}}$ and $0.002{\mathrm{~mM}}$ luminol in the buffers. We found that the signal-to-background ratio ( $\scriptstyle\mathrm{\backslashCL/}$ $\mathrm{CL}_{0.}^{\cdot}$ ) was very low at $\mathrm{\pH}<8,$ and rapidly increased with $\mathrm{pH}>$ 8, reaching a peak value at $\mathrm{pH}10\$ . This phenomenon agrees with those reported in the previous works and mechanism study.34,36 But, the CL dropped substantially at $\mathrm{pH}11$ and 12 (Figure 3b), which could be due to the significant damage to the MOF structure under the highly basic conditions. At $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ 10, the MOF produced a CL signal ${\dot{\sim}}10^{4}$ folds higher than that in the mixture of luminol/ $\mathrm{\mathrm{^{\prime}H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or luminol/Fe/Co-TPY-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ systems (Figure S8a). More interestingly, we found that CL production at this $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ could last for at least $1\ \mathrm{min}$ (Figure 3c). In contrast, the no-TPY MOF, Fe/Co-MIL$88\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ , only produced a burst of CL that decades rapidly within $20~\mathsf{s}.$ . The high stability of the TPY MOF, as well as the limited diffusion of the catalytic center and confined reactants within the porous MOF structures37,38 could both contribute to the strong and long-lasting luminescence emission, which is favorable for accurate and sensitive CL analysis.39,40 The optimal luminol concentration in the CL generation reaction was determined to be $0.032~\mathrm{mM}$ (Figure S8b).
We also tested whether the TPY MOF can be coupled with GOx to form an enzyme cascade for CL production (Figure 3a). Since the optimal CL signaling $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ is 10, to realize the one-pot reaction, $\mathbf{GOx}$ should still be active in this $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ . Thus, we evaluated the $\mathbf{GOx}$ activity at various $\mathrm{\pH}s$ by benzoquinone, which can replace $\mathrm{~O}_{2}$ as an electron acceptor in the oxidation of $\beta$ -D-glucose, and be converted to hydroquinone $\mathrm{(HQ)}$ to exhibit an absorbance peak at 289 nm (molar absorptivity (εHQ) = 2600 L·mol−1·cm−1).41,42 The absorbance at $289~\mathrm{nm}$ was monitored for $1~\mathrm{min}$ after mixing $0.00375~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ GOx with $0.025\%$ $\bf(w/v)$ benzoquinone and $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ glucose; and the initial reaction velocity was calculated using the Beer-Lamber law and the $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{HQ}}$ value reported. Agreeing with the common reaction conditions found in literature, $\mathrm{\pH}\ s$ gave the highest reaction rate, which decreased with increasing $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ (Figure 3b). More significant reaction rate drop was observed with $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ beyond 11 and 12; and at $\mathrm{\pH\10},$ the reaction rate remains to be about $86\%$ of the optimal value, supporting the feasibility of coupling both GOx and the MOF for CL production at $\mathrm{pH}10\$ .
At last, we tested the enzymatic cascade reaction by mixing $25~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ with $2.5~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ $\mathbf{GOx},$ $1.0\mathrm{\mM}$ glucose, and $0.032\mathrm{~mM}$ luminol in $10\mathrm{\mM}$ borate buffer at pH 10. CL production was monitored for $2~\mathrm{min}$ . The CL signal rapidly increased with time, but negligible CL was observed without MOF, without glucose or without $\mathbf{GOx}$ (Figure 3d). Similar to the long-lasting CL seen in Figure $^{3\mathrm{c},}$ at $\mathrm{\pH\10}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , the enzyme cascade also produced CL that reached a maximum emission at ${\sim}60~\mathrm{s}$ and maintained at ${\sim}50\%$ of the maximum intensity for at least $30~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure S9) with $25\mu\mathrm{g}/\$ mL MOF and $250~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ $\operatorname{GOx}$ in the reaction system. Under this cascade reaction condition, only $1\%$ Co and $2\%$ Fe were released by the MOF as found by ICP-AES (Table S2), confirming that the catalytic activity comes from the metal sites in the MOF rather than the freed metals. Agreeing with the $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ test results shown in Figure 3b, low CL was found from the enzyme cascade at other alkaline $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ values. To further confirm the feasibility of using the enzymatic cascade reaction for biosensing, we spiked different amounts of $\mathbf{GOx}$ into the mixture of $25~\mu\mathrm{g/mL}$ MOF, $\mathrm{~1~mM}$ glucose, and $0.032\ \mathrm{mM}$ luminol in $10\ \mathrm{mM}$ borate buffer at $\mathrm{pH}10_{\mathrm{;}}$ , and collected the CL signal at $1\mathrm{min}$ after mixing. The lowest GOx concentration detectible by the system was $\mathrm{\dot{0.15}{\ n M}}$ (Figure S10).
Detection of EVs in biological samples. The high stability and excellent peroxidase-like activity of Fe/Co-TPY$\mathrm{MIL}{-}8\dot{8}\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)$ should make it highly useful in biosensing. To prove this, we employed the MOF-based enzymatic cascade reaction for detection of EVs. EVs are considered as promising circulating tumor markers owing to their critical roles in cancer progressing and metastasis, the inherent stability of EVs in circulation, and the diversity of EV cargos.18,43,44 We modified the $\mathrm{Fe/Co\mathrm{-}T P Y\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}88(N H_{2})}$ with the anti-CD63 aptamer for the capture of exosomes, an EV subfamily considered to be valuable in cancer diagnosis and quenched the residual reactive groups after glutaraldehyde activation by the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -modified polyethylene glycol $\left(\mathrm{PEG-NH}_{2}\right),$ ) and glycine. The modification caused ${\sim}30\%$ decrease in the CL signal produced by the enzyme cascade, but still the plateau CL was ${>}30$ times higher than that obtained at $t=0$ s (Figure S11). We then optimized the conjugation conditions as well as the amount of the aptamer-conjugated MOF used for exosome binding (Figure S12). Once the exosomes are captured by the MOF, the biotinylated antibody recognizing another marker on the exosomeswould then bind to the streptavidin and biotinylated $\mathbf{GOx}$ to form the enzyme cascade for CL production (Figure 4a). Since the MOF can be quickly spun down using a common table-top centrifuge, it can be used to enrich the target into a small volume from a diluted sample before $\mathbf{GOx}$ binding: $99.92\%$ of the exosome particles spiked in $200\mu\mathrm{L}1\times$ PBS was captured by the MOF after $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ incubation. This isolation efficiency was comparable to that from the commercial, $_{1-\mu\mathrm{{m}}}$ magnetic beads modified by the same aptamer (Figure 4b).
We confirmed that the enzyme cascade can detect EVs dispersed in $\mathbf{\mathrm{1\timesPBS}}$ at concentrations ranging from $5\times10^{5}$ to $4\dot{\times}10^{7}$ particles/ $\mathrm{\DeltamL}$ (Figure 4c), using two surface markers, CD9 and HER2, for signaling, and capturing the EV subfamily, exosomes, using CD63. Such a detection range was about 1 order of magnitude wider than that $(10^{5}-10^{\overline{{6}}}$ particles $/_{\mathrm{mL}}$ ) obtained with the Fe/Co-MIL-88 $\left\langle{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}\right\rangle$ ) MOF,17 and the slope of the linear curve plotted between signal change ratio and Log (EV concentration) was about 2.3 times larger, indicating faster signal change with target input (Figure S13). LOD calculated with the $3\sigma$ method is $1.26\times10^{5}$ particles/ $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathrm{mL}}$ and $1.15\times10^{5}$ particles/ $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathrm{mL}}$ for using CD9 and HER2 as the detection marker, respectively (Figure 4c). These LODs are ${\sim}100$ times lower than the standard methods like ELISA and NTA for EV quantification.44,43 From Figure 4c, we can also see the overall signal from detecting HER2 is lower than from CD9, indicating the lower abundance of HER2 than CD9 on the EVs. This is reasonable because HER2 is a tumor protein, which may not be present in all exosomes, while CD9 is another exosomal marker just like CD63. However, we anticipate using the specific tumor protein would identify those exosomes derived from tumor cells, and could be more specific in cancer diagnosis.
We then applied the assay to detect exosomes in human sera collected from breast cancer patients and healthy people ( $\overset{\prime}{\boldsymbol{n}}=8$ for each cohort). The exosomes carrying both CD63 and CD9, or both CD63 and HER2, were tested. Serum incubation again did not lead to metal release from the MOF as confirmed by ICP-AES (Table S2). We found that the exosome concentrations in these clinical samples were much higher than the upper detection limit of our assay, and far less than $1\mu\mathrm{L}$ serum was needed for each measurement. The samples collected from BC patients contained significantly higher exosome concentrations than those from the healthy controls (Figure 5a). The CD63-HER2 combination showed a slightly lower $p$ value $\left(p=\right.$ 0.0087, Student’s t test) than that obtained with the CD63- CD9-based assay $\left(p=0.013\right)$ . These results support that Fe/ Co-TPY-MIL-88( $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) can enable sensitive biomarker detection in complex biological matrices with ultralow sample consumption and should possess high potential in clinical applications.
## CONCLUSIONS
In the present work, the Fe/Co-TPY-MIL-88 $\left\langle\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right\rangle$ MOF was constructed using the pore partition agent TPY to saturate the open metal site and enhance the stability of the MOF structure in bioassays. Addition of TPY does not affect the peroxidasemimicking activity of the MOF, and the resultant high stability in basic solutions permits it to work with $\mathbf{GOx}$ in the cascade enzymatic reactions to generate CL at a basic $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ of 10 by inputting glucose. When applied to detect a group of biomarkers, exosomes, a dynamic range 1 order of magnitude wider than that obtained with $\mathrm{Fe/Co.MIL}{\cdot}88(\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot})$ was obtained with a comparable LOD. These results support that, the high versatility and tunability of the MOF structures could bring in significant benefits to biosensing and enable ultrasensitive detection of biomarkers with judicious material designs. Future improvements in assay performance can consider reducing the hydration size of the MOF by enhancing its hydrophilicity and amplifying the number of $\mathbf{GOx}$ bound to the MOF mediated by the target. In addition, superior to typical CL systems, both the stand-alone peroxidase-mimicking activity of the $\mathrm{Fe/Co\mathrm{-}T P Y\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}88(N H_{2})}$ MOF and the cascade reaction system composed of the MOF and $\mathbf{GOx}$ can generate long-lasting CL. This property is worthy of further investigation for better understanding of the reaction mechanism and may benefit applications of such materials in areas other than biosensing, like light or energy production and biosynthesis.
